#!/bin/bash
# Setting up Ferry with Claude (plugin.conf's setup, run in this folder; Welcome to Nightdrive runs it after Tailscale's):
# Claude checks what stands in Ferry's way (`ferry list --json`'s setup step: Tailscale, the tailnet's HTTPS
# certificates, Tailscale's permission), helps clear it, and walks the phone through Add Device. The phone's page and
# the tailnet's admin console stay in the browser.
dir=$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$0")")
"$HOME/.config/hypr/scripts/claude-setup" ferry Ferry <<EOF
Set up Ferry with me, by $dir/README.md. First say in a few plain sentences what Ferry is for: a mailbox between this
computer and my phone, for photos, files, text and links both ways, from anywhere over Tailscale, with no app on the
phone (a page added to the Home Screen). Then run \`ferry list --json\` and read its "setup": null means Ferry is on my
tailnet; otherwise it names the first thing in the way, with what to do. Tailscale comes first: if it isn't logged in,
tell me to set up Tailscale in Welcome to Nightdrive first and stop there. If the tailnet's HTTPS certificates are off,
open https://login.tailscale.com/admin/dns for me and tell me to turn on HTTPS Certificates at the bottom of that page.
If Tailscale needs permission, give me \`! sudo tailscale set --operator=\$USER\` to run myself. After each fix run
\`ferry enable\`, then check \`ferry list --json\` again. Once "setup" is null, add my phone: run \`ferry app\` and tell
me to pick Add Device there, scan the QR code with the phone's camera, open the page, add it to the Home Screen
(Share › Add to Home Screen on an iPhone), open Ferry from there and tap Turn on notifications. Check that "push" in
\`ferry list --json\` grows to 1 or more. Finish by telling me where Ferry lives now: the inbox icon in the top bar
(its menu lists what my devices sent, and Aa, Send Clipboard and + send to the phone), and that files dropped on the
icon go to the phone.
EOF
